<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumara Kampana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kumara Kampana was a prince and general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukka-raya-i">Bukka Raya I</a>. In the 1360s he conquered the Sambuvaraya kingdom around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, and around 1370–1378 he destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a>, bringing the Tamil country under Vijayanagara. His wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangadevi">Gangadevi</a> celebrated these campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhura-vijayam">Madhura Vijayam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumara-kampana.jpg" alt="Kumara Kampana" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sastri, G Harihara; Sastri, V Srinivasa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madura_Vijayam_1924_Edition.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
